{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261111T155548Z-a21a8834", "ts": "2026-11-11T16:00:28Z", "actor": {"worktree": "cordyceps", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev", "branch": "feature/script-rebuild", "build": "fe0fee976e02"}, "body": {"base_commit": "6f4b99c717f3e44be912cc55d024141d02bd824c", "base_tree": "53f8998e77ef3a6d5c80c016078d1576ebf8aed0", "head_tree": "95fe51198e86ad0a7ee482ec91cc49a1867f92c8", "head_commit": "1f9f95ba33fb84d5226f710e6c9ee0e120d44a6e", "dispatch_commit": "1f9f95ba33fb84d5226f710e6c9ee0e120d44a6e", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["CHANGELOG.md", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/PortRemovalTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ScriptConfigurePlanTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ScriptProgramTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ScriptSignatureTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/PortRemoval.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptComponents.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptConfigurePlan.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptProgram.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptSignature.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/SetupScriptComponent.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Zoomable.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-script-rebuild.md", ".prawduct/changes/script-rebuild.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/Cordyceps.Tests.csproj", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/PortRemovalTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ScriptConfigurePlanTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ScriptProgramTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps.Tests/ScriptSignatureTests.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/PortRemoval.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptComponents.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptConfigurePlan.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptProgram.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptSignature.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/SetupScriptComponent.md", "src/Cordyceps/McpServer.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Zoomable.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-script-rebuild.md", ".prawduct/changes/script-rebuild.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Host behaviour behind the breaking refusal and the zoomable rebuild has no evidence beyond unit tests (VRF-022 pending)", "recommendation": "Unit tests cover only the host-free decisions (PortRemoval, ScriptSignature, ScriptConfigurePlan, ScriptProgram). Three things are checked only by reading the code: ToolHelpers.TryConfirmPortRemoval's re-resolution, which nicknames ObjectResolver treats as defaults for C#/Python/unified Script components (the refusal is built on this), and whether ScriptComponents.RebuildForChange after a zoomable reshape leaves the program matching the new ports. The plan records this as Level Medium until VRF-022 runs, and VRF-022 is still pending. Until it runs, a release could ship a breaking refusal whose premise nobody has watched work, and an agent would then get either wrong refusals or a stale program. Run VRF-022 steps 1-10 before the next release. Or accept this with a recorded reason, keeping VRF-022 as the gate on the release rather than on this merge. Instance.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptComponents.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Zoomable.cs"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: the port-removal prediction copies the mutation's route choice and port reading by hand, in untested host code", "recommendation": "The refusal (a breaking change shipped for safety) is right only while four hand-made copies agree. GhScriptTool.ConfigureRemoval repeats ActionConfigure's route condition (`ghComponent is IGH_VariableParameterComponent && anyParamSide`, else code-and-not-empty). SourceSyncRemoval repeats SyncScriptParams's guards (IGH_Component + IGH_VariableParameterComponent). The 'skip the built-in output 0' rule is written independently at four sites (SourceSyncRemoval and ConfigureRemoval `Output.Skip(1)`, SyncScriptParams `Skip(1)` + skipCount 1, SyncParamsForConfigure skipCount 1). All of it is in a host-coupled file that no test reaches. PortRemoval's remarks claim prediction and mutation 'cannot disagree', but they only share the pure ParamSyncPlan/ScriptSignature step; the inputs and route choice feeding that step are copies. The consequence: a maintainer who changes a sync guard, the built-in-output count, or adds a configure route without touching the matching *Removal helper makes the refusal under-predict, so a unique default nickname again picks the component whose wires get cut, and no test notices. This breaks the learnings rule 'Validate-then-mutate, and never conflate \"unparseable\" with \"empty\"' (\"New pure decision logic goes in a host-free `Core/` file linked into the test project WITH tests in the same chunk, even when it feels like host glue\"). ScriptComponents.RebuildForChange's expectation choice (written source / null-with-reason / stored source) is a second member of the class in this diff. Scope: class. Fix it by construction: add one host-free sync plan (inputs: existing input names, existing output names past the built-in output, whether the component is variable-parameter, the parsed defs, code). It returns the route, the per-side target names and the PortRemoval. TryConfirmPortRemoval and SyncParamSide both execute that one plan, and the skip-1 rule lives there once, with tests. To mechanize the rule: a check (record_lint or the test-evidence step) that every new `src/Cordyceps/Core/*.cs` is either linked in Cordyceps.Tests.csproj or listed in an explicit host-coupled allowlist with a reason. ScriptComponents.cs would then have to justify itself, and the split would come up before review.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/PortRemoval.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptComponents.cs"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "ActionConfigure tracks its outcome in six interdependent flags and calls the rebuild gate from three places", "recommendation": "This diff adds parametersChanged, parametersApplied and parametersError next to configured, codeSet, sourceError, writtenSource and a null-rebuildFields sentinel. parametersChanged is always equal to `parametersApplied == true`: both are set on the same line pair and nowhere else, so it is redundant. Three calls decide the rebuild: RebuildForChange in the param route, in the code-only route, and in the `rebuildFields == null` fallback. Each passes a different hand-built combination of those flags. `success` is computed from a fourth combination. The consequence: a new configure route, or an edit to one, has to set three or four flags consistently and pick the right gate call. For example, setting parametersApplied without parametersChanged would report the ports as applied while skipping the rebuild, and nothing would catch it because the method is host code. Scope: instance. Model the two halves directly: a parameter outcome (NotRequested / Applied / Refused(reason)) and a source outcome (NotRequested / Written(text) / Failed(reason)). Derive success, message, the codeSet/parametersApplied fields and ONE RebuildForChange call (made after the branches, before ExpireSolution) from that pair. Drop parametersChanged.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Inferences 1 and 2 are still marked inferred at the boundary review that ships them", "recommendation": "The build plan's Goals list 'Inferences (made on the owner's behalf; the boundary review shows them to the owner)'. Inference 1 answers #84 with 'rebuild and report' rather than 'refuse'. Inference 2 makes the refusal depend on the removal set rather than the action's name, and counts a rename as a removal. Both are built here, in GhCanvasTool.Zoomable.cs, GhScriptTool.cs and ToolHelpers.TryConfirmPortRemoval, and Inference 2 shapes a breaking change. The owner ruled only that removing a port is destructive; the semantics past that are the agent's. Consequence: the PR merges a breaking contract that the owner has not confirmed, and no record marks it as decided. Have the owner confirm or amend Inferences 1 and 2, record their words, and mark the Goals entries as ruled. Inference 3 (group_remove), which was put back to the owner, gets the same treatment. Scope: instance.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Zoomable.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ToolHelpers.cs"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "configure now refuses non-script components, a contract change with no consumer-impact evidence", "recommendation": "GhScriptTool.ActionConfigure now returns success:false for any component that is not a script. Before this branch, configure reshaped the inputs of Merge, Entwine and other variable-parameter components. boundary-patterns.md lists the MCP action contract as the primary external surface: 'Breaking any of these breaks installed users' agent workflows ... never silent renames or removals.' The plan's mid-build decision justifies the refusal by the no-pre-existing-exception rule, but it records no look at who calls configure on those components. CHANGELOG.md files the change under Fixed, not as Breaking, and the change fragment's Consumer note leaves it out entirely. That note also says 'Calls that only add or keep ports are unaffected', which is false for a configure on a Merge that only adds inputs. Consequence: an agent workflow that calls configure on a Merge breaks after upgrade with no consumer note warning of it. Record the consumer-impact decision. If the change is breaking, list it with the other breaking change and in the consumer note. Scope: instance.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "CHANGELOG.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Build plan cites .prawduct/owner-rulings.md, which is on neither this branch nor develop", "recommendation": "Records Pass, bar 'misleads into action'. The plan's Goals ('Response taken: proceed, citing the owner's rulings of 2026-11-11 (.prawduct/owner-rulings.md)') name that file as the authority for this work, including the breaking #107 refusal. The file exists only on the unmerged chore/records-catchup branch, added in 6a89650, which is an ancestor of neither HEAD nor develop. Methodology building.md 'When the Owner Rules' asks for the owner's words to be written to .prawduct/owner-rulings.md, and the template asks for it to be committed 'with the work it governs'. Consequence: a reviewer or later agent checking why a breaking change was authorised finds no ruling on develop. If this PR merges first, or records-catchup is abandoned, the citation stays dangling and the merge-order dependency is recorded nowhere. Carry the rulings with this branch, or record the ordering dependency in the plan. Record-lint did not catch this because the citation is in Goals, not in a chunk section. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-script-rebuild.md"], "fid": "R-6"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 6, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Python detection reads only the caller's code, not the directive the tool preserves from stored source", "recommendation": "ScriptSignature.Parse and PortRemoval.ForSourceSync receive the raw `code` (ActionSet, and the code-only configure route through SyncScriptParams). The language, though, comes from finalSource = PreserveLanguageDirective(component, code). The tips document relying on that preservation ('Script language is preserved automatically'). Take a unified Script component that already stores `#! python 3`. A directive-less Python body whose text contains `RunScript(` without a `def` before it (a comment such as `# RunScript(x, y) is called by GH`, or a call) fails IsPythonSource. The C# regex then matches it, finds no two-token parameters, and declares zero inputs, so the sync removes every input. The prediction agrees with the sync, so the default-nickname refusal fires, but the same call by GUID still cuts every input wire. The SDK-mode `def RunScript` case the fix targeted is handled correctly. Fix at the instance: compute finalSource before the confirmation (it is a pure read) and pass it to SourceSyncRemoval and to both SyncScriptParams call sites. Prediction and mutation then still read one text. Add a ScriptSignatureTests case where the directive comes only from preservation.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptSignature.cs"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "set_count refusal can name ports the call would not actually remove", "recommendation": "PortRemoval.ForSetCount asks the component only about the last port, then reports every port past targetCount. SetParameterCount's loop asks CanRemoveParameter again at each new last index and stops at the first refusal, so for a component with a minimum count (for example `set_count count=0` on a Merge) the refusal text ('This call removes inputs ...') names ports the call would have kept. Whether the call is destructive is unaffected, because the set is non-empty either way. Only the description overstates. If it is worth fixing, walk from the end with canRemove until the first refusal (or targetCount), so the description matches the loop. Instance.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/PortRemoval.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhCanvasTool.Zoomable.cs"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "configure's new refusal of non-script components is a contract-surface removal labelled as a fix", "recommendation": "An inputs-only `gh_script configure` on a Merge or another variable-parameter component used to work: the input side uses skipCount 0, and only the output side misplaced ports. It is now refused. The decision is recorded in the plan's mid-build decisions. However, CHANGELOG lists it under Fixed with no 'Breaking:' prefix, and neither the change fragment's consumer note nor the plan's Release paragraph mentions it. Meanwhile boundary-patterns.md says the MCP action contract never changes by silent removal. gh_script's help already scoped it to script components, so the use was off-label and the impact is small. Either mark it Breaking where the port-removal refusal is marked, or record why off-label use is not covered by the contract. Instance.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The rebuild gate's boundary does not match its documentation: set bypasses it, and its decision type is still named and documented for configure only", "recommendation": "ScriptComponents.RebuildForChange describes itself as 'The single gate for every route that reshapes a script component'. But ActionSet, which reshapes ports through SyncScriptParams and writes source, calls the public ScriptComponents.RebuildAndVerify directly. The two agree today only because set always writes source. Meanwhile ScriptConfigurePlan is now the decision behind gh_canvas zoomable too, yet its name and its summary ('Host-free decision for gh_script(action=configure)') still scope it to configure. A maintainer adding a route reads 'configure' and 'single gate' and can end up putting the new decision in the wrong place. Scope: instance. Route set through RebuildForChange(component, parametersChanged, finalSource), make RebuildAndVerify private, and either give the plan a tool-neutral name (e.g. ScriptRebuildPlan) or drop the 'configure' scoping from its summary.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptComponents.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptConfigurePlan.cs", "src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Script language is now decided from source in a second place, ScriptSignature.IsPythonSource, which copies ScriptDirective's directive rule", "recommendation": "ScriptDirective owns the Rhino 8 language-specifier rule ('#!' = Python, '// #!' = C#, BOM-trimmed first line), but keeps the Python-vs-C# split inside ExtractDirective. The 1f9f95b fix rebuilt that split in ScriptSignature.IsPythonSource (ExtractDirective, then its own BOM trim and StartsWith(\"#!\")), next to a type-name 'Python' check that repeats IsScriptComponent's heuristic. The defect it fixed came from one place deciding the language by itself (type name only). A third independent decider is how the next divergence happens. Parse also reads the caller's `code`, not the directive-preserved finalSource that is actually stored. Scope: instance. Expose the language from ScriptDirective (e.g. a ScriptDirective.Language(source) returning Python/CSharp/null) and have ScriptSignature ask it, ideally about the final source.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Core/ScriptSignature.cs"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: #107 is answered by this branch but not listed in closes", "recommendation": "#107 ('names: decide whether removing a port counts as destructive') is open in the cache. This branch decides and implements it, but the change fragment's closes: lists only #84. The one part still open is the group_remove confirmation from Inference 3. Record which applies: #107 closes with this PR, or it stays open for group_remove, saying so on the item.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/script-rebuild.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: #64 (GHS-3W9N) shipped but is still open", "recommendation": "#64 ('gh-script: configure destroys all wires instead of preserving') is open in the cache. migration-scrub-decisions.md maps GHS-3W9N to #64 with status 'shipped', and CHANGELOG.md documents the LCS-preserving configure sync that fixed it. This branch rewrites that sync's surroundings and deleted the code comments that cited GHS-3W9N, so the item lies directly in the touched area. It looks stale and can be closed.", "files": ["src/Cordyceps/Tools/Unified/GhScriptTool.cs"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Learnings cross-check: applicable rules honoured", "recommendation": "Read core.md, release.md and tests.md, the list from learnings-files --for-diff. 'Validate-then-mutate, and never conflate unparseable with empty': the removal set, destructive re-resolution and non-script refusal all run before the first mutation, and the new decision logic (ScriptConfigurePlan, PortRemoval, ScriptSignature) is host-free, linked in Cordyceps.Tests.csproj and tested. 'Code linked into Cordyceps.Tests must stay host-free': ScriptComponents, which logs, stays unlinked. 'Tracing consumers of a shared helper misses paths that bypass it': every UnregisterInput/OutputParameter site (zoomable remove, zoomable set_count, SyncParamSide) is gated. 'Never offer the user a choice you have not verified': GetServerInstructions, ActionInfo, the [Description] for id, README and the guides all list the port removals. 'Build-plan ## Status checkboxes are hand-ticked': all three are unticked, which is correct to hold for a cumulative-final plan until this review passes. No violations found.", "oid": "O-8"}], "duration_seconds": 720, "scope": "script-rebuild", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "6f4b99c717f3e44be912cc55d024141d02bd824c", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-script-rebuild.md", ".prawduct/changes/script-rebuild.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "README.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CanvasOutlineGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/CommonErrorsGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/GettingStartedGuide.md", "src/Cordyceps/Knowledge/Prompts/SetupScriptComponent.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02", "03"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-script-rebuild.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-11-11T15:55:48Z"}}

